  "live_records": {
    "q-84459": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84459",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,6)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,6),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-(-2)=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
