    "q-84821": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84821",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14162956/assets/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
