    "q-106454": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106454",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-2,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-10,-5)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,-5),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=-5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-3=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
