    "q-323132": {
      "dom_id": "step-q323132",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-1=3t,\\,y+4={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-1}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y+4$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =y+4 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =9(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
