    "q-321592": {
      "dom_id": "step-q321592",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
