    "q-323212": {
      "dom_id": "step-q323212",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-4=3{t}^{2},\\,y+2=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x-4}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y+2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+2}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x-4}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x-4}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =36\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =12(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
